DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://dev:dev@db:5432/app
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://dev:dev@db:5432/app_test
ADMIN_TOKEN=dev-secret-change-me
TMDB_API_KEY=dev-tmdb-key
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Credentials for the OpenAI-compatible providers the gateway can reach. **Production runs all
# four stages on DeepInfra** (ADR-0012), so `DEEPINFRA_API_KEY` is required there; locally it
# is optional and unset is fine while the code defaults stay Anthropic. Also what the offline
# verification and eval scripts need (`scripts/verify_provider_capabilities.py`). The gateway
# refuses to run a stage whose provider has no key rather than falling back to Anthropic, which
# would attribute one provider's results to another.
# DEEPINFRA_API_KEY=
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# Optional: which provider answers each stage, and on which model. `anthropic` (the code
# default) | `deepinfra` | `deepseek` — an unrecognized *name* fails the container at boot, and
# so does a recognized one with no credential or no rates entry for its `(provider, model)`
# pair (NEU-981). The model stays a separate setting per stage: a provider and a model id are
# not interchangeable, and `(provider, model)` is what pricing is keyed on.
#
# The code defaults are Anthropic and stay that way — a test or a script with no environment
# should not reach for a network provider. Production overrides them in
# `docker-compose.prod.yml`; the values it uses are shown below.
# LINK_PROVIDER=deepinfra
# LINK_MODEL=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash
# CLUSTER_PROVIDER=deepinfra
# CLUSTER_MODEL=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro
# SOURCE_JUDGE_PROVIDER=deepinfra
# SOURCE_JUDGE_MODEL=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash
# SUMMARY_PROVIDER=deepinfra
# SUMMARY_MODEL=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash
# RESOLVE_PROVIDER=deepinfra
# RESOLVE_MODEL=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro
# Optional: retrieval tuning — the minimum title-token score a film needs (0..1) and the cap
# on candidates shown per story. The defaults below were tuned against 98,662 production
# stories at a 1,226-film catalog (NEU-1001); they stay settings so the next catalog
# expansion is a config change rather than a deploy.
# LINK_RETRIEVAL_THRESHOLD=0.5
# LINK_RETRIEVAL_MAX_CANDIDATES=25
# Optional: the retrieval-health hard breach (NEU-1002). A `link` run whose zero-candidate
# rate exceeds the ceiling finalizes `failed` — which aborts the daily chain and pings the
# healthchecks.io deadman, the pipeline's only alerting channel. The rate
# is ignored below the minimum story count, so a quiet news day cannot trip it. Set the
# ceiling to 1.0 to disarm the guard without a deploy.
# LINK_RETRIEVAL_MAX_ZERO_CANDIDATE_RATE=0.25
# LINK_RETRIEVAL_HEALTH_MIN_STORIES=50
# Optional: person resolution (D-21, NEU-1363). The floor is the lowest score a candidate may
# accept on; the margin is how far clear of the runner-up it must be, and is what keeps two
# same-named people routing to the `tiebreak` queue instead of one of them being picked. Both
# ship untuned — the first weeks of `/admin/resolution` are the corpus that tunes them, which
# is why they are settings. `RESOLVE_MENTIONS_PER_RUN` is a TMDB request budget as much as a
# work limit (one `/search/person` per uncached mention); the remainder is the next run's
# backlog, not a loss. `RESOLVE_ENABLED=false` skips the pass entirely for a TMDB outage.
# The `tiebreak` queue itself is answered by the `resolve` gateway stage (D-22, NEU-1364),
# whose `(provider, model)` is configured with the other stages above — it is the only stage
# that is not called on every unit of its pass, so widening the margin above buys model calls
# and narrowing it buys unreviewed deterministic accepts.
# RESOLVE_ACCEPT_FLOOR=0.5
# RESOLVE_ACCEPT_MARGIN=0.12
# RESOLVE_MENTIONS_PER_RUN=500
# RESOLVE_ENABLED=true
# Optional: the dormancy window (ADR-0015). An undated film with no `catalog.film_field_change`
# row and no linked story for this many days leaves `active_film_clause` — and with it the
# retrieval index, the per-film query list, and the sweep's seed-person query. Any later change
# or linked story revives it; nothing is ever deleted. The default is a deliberately long
# placeholder that retires almost nothing; the M4 tuning ticket sets the real value from the
# discovery probe, which is why this is a setting and not a constant.
# SWEEP_DORMANCY_DAYS=365

# Optional: how often the sweep's refresh phase still re-fetches a *dormant* film (project
# spec §4.5). Dormancy is a reduced cadence here, not an exemption: detecting the change
# that revives a film requires reading it, so a dormancy that stopped the refresh would be
# a one-way door. Another placeholder the M4 tuning ticket sets — erring short only costs
# requests, erring long delays every revival by that much.
# SWEEP_DORMANT_REFRESH_DAYS=30

# Optional: how far back the sweep's field-change phase reads `catalog.film_field_change`
# for release-date and status changes to card (ADR-0014). A fixed rolling window, not a
# watermark — re-reading a change already carded is a no-op, so the overlap is what keeps a
# failed sweep from losing a day's changes. It is also the day-one guard: the table holds
# months of history, and without a floor the first pass after deploy would card every date
# move ever recorded.
# SWEEP_EVENT_LOOKBACK_DAYS=7

# Optional: the sweep's master switch and its per-tranche admission flags (project spec
# §7.3, §7.4). SWEEP_ENABLED=false lets the sweep run and report without admitting anything —
# the one-move rollback. It gates *admission* only: the refresh and events phases still run,
# because refreshing a film the catalog already holds, and carding a change to it, admit
# nothing — and gating them would mean the shipped defaults never produce a catalog event at
# all. The three tranche flags ramp admission by seed grade, one at a
# time, so the retrieval-health guard sees three steps instead of one cliff; opening one is
# an env change. All four default to false however far the ramp has got: directors opened in
# production on 2026-08-11 (NEU-1086), and writers (NEU-1089) and cast (NEU-1090) are both
# supported paths, but none of the three shows up as a changed default here. Cast roughly
# doubles the catalog and is expected to breach the retrieval soft tier when it opens, by
# design. This file is the local template; production opens a
# tranche as a Coolify variable, since SWEEP_ADMIT_* appear in no compose file. Open the next
# one only once retrieval health is green on the last: that sequencing is what makes a
# precision drop attributable to one seed grade.
# SWEEP_ENABLED=false
# SWEEP_ADMIT_DIRECTORS=false
# SWEEP_ADMIT_WRITERS=false
# SWEEP_ADMIT_CAST=false

# Optional: how many distinct seed people must reach an undated film before it may be
# admitted (project spec §4.1). One director attachment is the earliest signal the product
# sells and is also what a speculative TMDB entry looks like (§4.2), so this is the dial
# between the two, and the M4 tuning ticket sets it from the discovery probe. The default is
# the identity value — the bar stays status plus a seed-grade role on the candidate film —
# so that opening the first tranche moves one dial and not two.
# SWEEP_CORROBORATION_THRESHOLD=1

# scripts/refresh_db.sh (task db:refresh) — pull prod data into the local database over SSH
# + docker exec. `task db:refresh` restores catalog/news/ingest only and leaves local app
# and user data alone; the app-schema variants prompt before dropping your local admin.
# Required:
#   PROD_SSH=user@host   SSH target where the prod Postgres container runs.
# Optional overrides:
#   PROD_PG_CONTAINER    Pin the prod container. Recommended: auto-discovery matches on
#                        catalog.film + the four schemas, and errors rather than guessing if
#                        more than one container matches, but Coolify names change on every
#                        redeploy so a pin needs re-checking after one. The host runs
#                        several Postgres containers under Coolify with UUID names that
#                        change on redeploy, which is why discovery matches on content
#                        rather than on a name or image tag.
#   PROD_PG_USER         Defaults to the container's POSTGRES_USER.
#   PROD_PG_DB           Defaults to the container's POSTGRES_DB. Coolify commonly leaves
#                        this at `postgres` with the app's schemas created on top.
#   LOCAL_PG_CONTAINER  Defaults to the workspace compose's `db` container, discovered
#                       through compose rather than by name.
#   LOCAL_DB            Defaults to app.
#   LOCAL_DB_USER       Defaults to dev.
#   FORCE=1              Skip the confirmation prompt on the app-schema variants.
PROD_SSH=
# Optional: enable Sentry error reporting. Leave unset to disable (default in local dev).
# Use the backlotter-backend project DSN from Sentry (org neuroticsasquatch).
# SENTRY_DSN=
# Optional: healthchecks.io deadman ping URLs for the Coolify scheduled tasks that run
# `python -m upmovies.pipeline_run daily|hourly|sweep`. Leave unset in local dev (ping
# no-ops). The sweep has its own check because it runs on its own slot, ~2h ahead of daily,
# and is deliberately not a stage in that chain (spec §6.1).
# HEALTHCHECK_DAILY_URL=
# HEALTHCHECK_HOURLY_URL=
# HEALTHCHECK_SWEEP_URL=

# Optional: transactional mail (D-30, NEU-1338). `noop` (the code default) records every send
# and logs it, transmitting nothing — which is what a local checkout and the test suite want,
# and what keeps a deploy with no Resend account booting. `resend` is the real provider, and
# switching to it makes the two settings below required: a missing key or a `MAIL_FROM` that
# is not an address fails the container at boot rather than the signup that needed the mail.
# MAIL_FROM takes either `a@b.co` or `Name <a@b.co>`; the domain must have SPF/DKIM/DMARC set
# up and be verified in Resend, or sends come back 422.
# MAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# MAIL_FROM=Backlotter <no-reply@backlotter.com>
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# Optional: what mail templates call the product, and how long an emailed verification link
# stays good (NEU-1339). Both carry usable code defaults — "Backlotter" and 24 hours — so
# neither has to be set; PRODUCT_NAME is here because a staging environment sending mail that
# calls itself the production product is how a test send gets mistaken for a real one, and the
# TTL is here because it is also mail *copy*: the template tells the reader how many hours
# they have, from this value.
# PRODUCT_NAME=Backlotter (staging)
# VERIFY_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=24
# How long an emailed password-reset link stays good (NEU-1340). Much shorter than the
# verification window on purpose: a spent verification token stamps a column, a spent reset
# token sets the password and drops every session, so it is the account. Also mail copy.
# RESET_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=1

# How long an emailed email-change confirmation stays good (NEU-1341). Matches the reset
# window rather than the verification one: spending this link moves the address the account
# signs in and recovers with, so it is reset-grade, not verify-grade. Also mail copy.
# EMAIL_CHANGE_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=1

# Open signup (D-18, NEU-1343). Cloudflare Turnstile's *secret* key — the server-side half of
# the widget pair, never the site key, which is public and belongs to the frontend. Unlike
# every optional setting above, an unset value here does not fall back to something workable:
# it is the whole of what stops `POST /auth/signup` being a script's endpoint now that an
# invite code is no longer required, so signup answers 503 until it is set. Local development
# and the test suite use the one value that verifies without calling Cloudflare.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET=dev-bypass

# The rollback switch for open signup (D-18). False does not close signup — it puts the invite
# code back in front of it, which is the state the route was in before NEU-1343, with comped
# invites still working and Turnstile still verified either way.
# SIGNUP_OPEN=true

# Per-IP rate limiting (D-19, NEU-1344). Every bucket carries a usable code default, so none
# of these has to be set; they are documented here because the two flags are the ones a local
# checkout actually wants to move. Each bucket is `"<capacity>/<refill_per_minute>"` — the
# burst a single address may spend at once, then the sustained rate underneath it, so
# `5/0.083` reads as "five now, five an hour". A malformed value fails the container at boot.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
# The public bucket ships **off**, and stays off until the SSR Worker signs its requests: the
# site is server-rendered on Cloudflare, so until then every anonymous visitor arrives from a
# handful of shared egress IPs and one bucket would throttle all of them together.
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_ENABLED=false
# RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP=5/0.083
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN=20/1.33
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_REQUEST=5/0.083
# RATE_LIMIT_IMPORT=6/0.1
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC=240/120
# RATE_LIMIT_ICS=30/30

# Where themoviedb.org sends the user back after they approve the TMDB import's request token
# (D-16, NEU-1357). A **frontend** page, not a route here: TMDB appends `request_token` and
# `approved` to it, and the page forwards them to `POST /me/import/tmdb/callback` with the
# session cookie and the CSRF header that route requires — which a bare cross-site redirect
# into the API would carry neither of. The code default points at the local frontend, so a
# local checkout needs nothing; production sets it to the deployed page.
# TMDB_REDIRECT_URL=https://backlotter.com/welcome?tmdb=callback

# The shared secret the SSR Worker signs its API calls with (NEU-1389), sent as
# `X-Backlotter-Origin`; a request carrying it may name the visitor it is rendering for in
# `X-Backlotter-Client-IP`, and that address is what the limiter meters instead of the
# Worker's egress IP. Unset means the headers are ignored — never that a signed request is
# refused — so a wrong or stale secret costs bucketing accuracy, not availability. Must match
# the Wrangler secret of the same name.
# SSR_ORIGIN_SECRET=
